#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198082 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "eae94514-cd92-5405-90b1-ee09dad18e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-13162" :question/problem "Sketch a representation of the set $S = \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{N}$ in the Cartesian plane." :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS = \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{N}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS=\n\\Big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z,\\, y\\in \\mathbb N\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_1\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any integer number and whose $y$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}{1}}.$\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\Big\\{ (x, {\\color{red}{1}}) \\: : \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_1$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/6b63e973bbcc3e25d601a1f6cef97f5a00cc22e738c46d04a1ac06851421894e.png)\n\nNext, consider $S_2\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any integer number and whose $y$-coordinate equals ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\Big\\{ (x,{\\color{blue}{2}}) \\: : \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_2$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/f24ce8f7610d096c795a8a74b0b714b720c36442042c28b66432d6b2102fb9d4.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $S_3,S_4,S_5,\\ldots,$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup {S}_{4}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{N}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/c999c61d8ffbb27d47272811e32340cef3253b4730e684be10615487a8b27200.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198097 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "bb40c174-3739-50df-835e-5d47c9902d5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13192" :question/problem "Sketch a representation of the set $S = \\{1, 2\\} \\times (0, 1)$ in the Cartesian plane." :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS = \\{1,2\\} \\times (0,1)\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $1$ or $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\nS=\n\\Big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in \\{{\\color{red}{1}},{\\color{blue}{2}}\\},\\, y\\in (0,1) \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_1\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}{1}}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\Big\\{ ({\\color{red}{1}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in(0,1) \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/56f200fce557749a56b9b5b51bc670da5f8c7f2cdb0196392ee80efcd6be980a.png)\n\nNext, consider $S_2\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\big\\{ ({\\color{blue}{2}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in(0,1) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/f96074cc265bba6eb59717364d2aa5390fdccea4d398fe5ae0b99f7ad82f0b93.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS = S_1\\cup S_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/0c0529aaa680b4b572d17f2cfbfb76d805134e72c50ab3ed469286cc025185a3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198133 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "aa337681-5d8c-5ea1-b0fe-5d8cd15040a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13320" :question/problem "Sketch a representation of the set $\\,\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane given that $I = [-1,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = \\{ i \\} \\times [0, \\, i^2].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x \\: : \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\nX_{-0.5} = \\{ -0.5 \\} \\times [0, 0.25] = \\big\\{ (-0.5,k) \\: : \\: k \\in [0, 0.25] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5, 0)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.25)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/ccc75eac899efcab1d0b6fde12609a3ea8f61d6fec74c739ef3393fad32645a9.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = \\{ i \\} \\times [0, i^2] = \\big\\{ (i,k) \\: : \\: k \\in [0, i^2] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i, i^2).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/60c98be5d97748e9285bb5e9c30bba9781a5ef99245789718a7828b18a6df8fb.png)\n\n \n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union $\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/b3a61d686c78c76a4f461c1c771437d31030339b634661da8b8dc4f492db55eb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198134 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "67569060-2f33-5a8d-ae73-690742d3d318" :question/math-academy-id "e-13321" :question/problem "Let $I = [0,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1].$ Sketch a representation of $\\displaystyle \\bigcap_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x \\: : \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for all}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\nX_{0.5} = [-0.5,0] \\times [-1, 1] = \\big\\{ (a,b) \\: : \\: a \\in [-0.5, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(-0.5, -1),$ $(-0.5, 1),$ $(0, 1),$ and $(0, -1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8ad650cb3f2fa4da920a442e8cc1b24af5027595cba4e43138eff2126cf4c102.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1] = \\big\\{ (a,b) \\: : \\: a \\in [-i, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(-i,-1),$ $(-i,1),$ $(0, 1),$ and $(0, -1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/a37cfa508cf3a60536084fbc1e57c5aeac3414904d2ad72ef1deda85dc4eddd8.png)\n\n \n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $\\{0\\}\\times [-1,1]{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/5e9a611629f4370c57e77a057439c759670afa17c14c4b2339e1d471f616ea72.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "132f77bd-9ddf-5caf-96a1-e220936f45d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307934} {:answer/id #uuid "098babed-40a5-5df9-8de1-0a88c19800f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/df4a1ecffe6302ee57250944ec8f178f46a066b2edff2ed1afb200e7b0217cf7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307935} {:answer/id #uuid "715d6dad-5b8d-5a07-ab6f-cc9c2db51162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/6b5b39546eabacdf948bd87e793727fdc8cd8f2664df037be53bfb630fc41670.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307936} {:answer/id #uuid "280a367a-db86-510d-83e6-8f1354018cdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/506d86848903de9f038d541152aabf67d99d0f4d0a7e5f5772b811ff1cf9dade.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307937} {:answer/id #uuid "51ec42ac-80bd-51b7-9c6b-1a00dd8a1002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/17a176a0e7cb254483d93741cd2c1e3db581005f479928ac36809e55bc970c07.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "132f77bd-9ddf-5caf-96a1-e220936f45d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307934} :answer-field/id #uuid "dda8c1e1-bad9-5b1c-b928-a0d2962fffd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30b7b0c8-79fe-53ad-bb30-601d2f7e078d" :question/math-academy-id "q-172690" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}={1}\\times [0,1]={(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/71dd97607df48caf579c2e476520507ede035f65c336064f022cd900b6c120fc.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,i]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4b87c830620f54e5b3a3bb7923be971b4d9d4abb98cfb3dcdc8387f376a0b452.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6e36dc9-ca9d-5871-ac65-5a8e2fd56e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/a2786a8cfb41d8fb352c95dc36688fb2eff4773680be54be9dd3d1c696ae588a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307941} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb187e0-61a4-5b94-8c71-8517589c46d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307942} {:answer/id #uuid "46ae9087-5cf5-516b-81da-46b3beae2ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4fa495b2b7e5528f5aa70abe8ce778fc0c1e7d5bcd4a9b855a72ee1469ed5f01.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307943} {:answer/id #uuid "3452be50-9b3a-545e-9265-22146b078969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/3b3d7d98bdc1c336c9151bff06f7d07bea7e2228efb253b0fc16cdd2de1faa99.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307944} {:answer/id #uuid "1e664514-cba7-564e-8f55-c3cbd248245f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/e5ed1251cb899e92d9f2e7dd6111140b07556432b5240fa5a9efacd33ce19989.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfb187e0-61a4-5b94-8c71-8517589c46d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307942} :answer-field/id #uuid "dead5edd-27f4-5ebd-8ce8-6749e3e0cbe8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "280b10a4-5dc0-5bb2-b73b-6d6eeefafeca" :question/math-academy-id "q-175295" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times {-i}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=[0,1]\\times {-1}={(k,-1)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(1,-1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/a39443fd1b3764ff60c0c052085134ad7eeda6759c07c4d936fc03cd121f72dd.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times {-i}={(k,-i)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(0,-i)$ to the point $(i,-i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/7f18af14c55acc4bc2cd63b601dba0bde3cd51c0284b398b401c854537003926.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd15d26e-bdf3-5fe0-84b9-980b25f01709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8ce0c60aafe1a41b372c1f1477241c584d6f8ae224113c360576feb37fc25a8c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307955} {:answer/id #uuid "129832fc-dc2b-57ac-840e-63de2e90981a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/fcda8aaa1da0ee1e9fee4957246497a927b4373c51d8a46db429540ea51f5083.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307956} {:answer/id #uuid "1231c5af-cdb2-54d0-952b-de9c2a8060ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/cd35c57fa9c6ce3ae2ee5961b8ae3e7d16ea1a2aa44c302a9e40f19b6c1b11ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307957} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fe220a-a262-58d6-ac9b-4071188d639e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/b6518f0f49f1147742d621fa940d49f84e7cce9c17d071fdfeb4a2e96abbf591.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307958} {:answer/id #uuid "d217b59c-798f-51ff-abfb-5b1969eb6aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/310ba014b846afb8d4f2bff610e762da71b47e8c4daa2d6ffebf4b62ecc4d1db.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd15d26e-bdf3-5fe0-84b9-980b25f01709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8ce0c60aafe1a41b372c1f1477241c584d6f8ae224113c360576feb37fc25a8c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307955} :answer-field/id #uuid "39d5bd33-5d17-5469-8cbe-cdc0b0c554f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15afbaad-5b0e-58d9-8df7-ceeef530333d" :question/math-academy-id "q-177652" :question/problem "Let $I=[-1,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [-|i|,|i|].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{-0.5}={-0.5}\\times [-0.5,0.5]={(-0.5,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5,-0.5)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/b7793d85e02ccd05c55c34971c90be087d58274eabd21f46fe09f08ccd60797b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [-|i|,|i|]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-|i|,|i|]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,-|i|)$ to the point $(i,|i|).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/c30712295adfe51c655662cc09f8f1e23b3ea97573693154e0e0b6ba48e15c07.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8ce0c60aafe1a41b372c1f1477241c584d6f8ae224113c360576feb37fc25a8c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95fdd016-0bad-5595-b4a6-0749069f92b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307969} {:answer/id #uuid "a0af478e-12ba-5c4d-bde3-b3f6ca1ed4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/664f327f59e76cb22e1b9bd0a83f84c24e75ad6a0d176532437f629bf80bfc29.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307970} {:answer/id #uuid "b320fcd3-9039-5aa1-b7d8-01e0cf9387c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307971} {:answer/id #uuid "b44fbb1b-82be-5d2a-a104-74bf816fc6af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4ddec65745de14217112f6365d9dd372df5a24a13ef8bedc8db592c9ee18cab1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307972} {:answer/id #uuid "cea7954c-3904-52ec-9cb4-2bf067d4efe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/05323cccad18bb4664fe74112ff312f53ef3e12016be6f7b30ca9146f2ba6119.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b320fcd3-9039-5aa1-b7d8-01e0cf9387c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307971} :answer-field/id #uuid "542d5677-3b19-503d-ac3a-c335de48d318" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef5d7cb6-447b-5fde-822f-0d2b0fd3c5e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-181777" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=R\\times Z$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times Z\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in Z}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $-1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(x,-1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8e677c814756c4818c25da450d789c974787df60cf4bb18017a96593d3ecc180.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/f99beeb00c070048ee4ee7c1b6c9e077d7d4e0f3fd9e63aa12b5b58588e14dbb.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times Z & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times Z$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd1d32a9-cfd5-529f-a9c5-4124d3fff3d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181949-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195394} {:answer/id #uuid "f62eb4a7-4e5a-5855-b06e-55459d1747fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181949-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195395} {:answer/id #uuid "1ecc40f6-f148-5be7-99e4-0e62de82948f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181949-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195396} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff1c24b-4180-5408-854f-65dae85655ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181949-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195397} {:answer/id #uuid "30580768-dd97-5527-b3ff-730be3c9de92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181949-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ff1c24b-4180-5408-854f-65dae85655ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181949-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195397} :answer-field/id #uuid "129a7ac0-6839-5c07-b5fd-fbb809ec9bf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24f0a183-4542-54dd-8464-0891330af854" :question/math-academy-id "q-181949" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={0,1,2}\\times {-1,1}$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing ${0,1,2}\\times {-1,1}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS={(0,-1),(0,1),(1,-1),(1,1),(2,-1),(2,1)}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/c5fdae9b803df3873ee7deef4a36678e0d8274dd256fe331360261f1fe6dd492.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cac57808-fb29-58a9-9a51-a3760b4bd0d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8de1a050eed07445dd4a80be2809e275419238f72e098693bfc1642a9e714403.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307983} {:answer/id #uuid "a88b7a69-8601-51e5-9537-bb47c7344882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/2df87b90d770a5f2c0ba40a55330409d1cd80866b7e34299eab6dcbf675c6a13.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307984} {:answer/id #uuid "23046aa2-d99e-59b1-8d64-e4267ed8eb2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/de19fcf0f246b09d4bb957568e026b36de3f55c1a6c7065d79faacee794a8326.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307985} {:answer/id #uuid "8c233025-4185-5730-914f-157508544a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/463ae2387423a48dd557af8cfd9b01b6bc08b95f1b3c2f337c6b0c53d3731fa2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307986} {:answer/id #uuid "99819f3d-1733-5f29-936f-25f43d428cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/80c13426f812f1e98b0ffd61d81eeb7c70a04c5013625d6e6d2add5f2d4a6d85.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23046aa2-d99e-59b1-8d64-e4267ed8eb2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/de19fcf0f246b09d4bb957568e026b36de3f55c1a6c7065d79faacee794a8326.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307985} :answer-field/id #uuid "74dbbc93-a894-5a25-a86e-42a3bfff7df8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "241c59ff-21cb-5099-884b-4ec060248a86" :question/math-academy-id "q-181966" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=Z\\times Z$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times Z\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/9673951b4e72ba503e740369462f9e528e43ffc083706ee558ade7ccd52702f5.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}={(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/797b66a293eb241f841869abe97e324f4301907f71029acceefd68487bef1b4f.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup \\text{ }{S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times Z$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/de19fcf0f246b09d4bb957568e026b36de3f55c1a6c7065d79faacee794a8326.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db693b49-2bec-59ba-91d8-3f26a5d1d9dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195461} {:answer/id #uuid "123dada4-b1fa-58e4-9523-a85c56398af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195462} {:answer/id #uuid "34a7d35a-8582-569d-a279-cf93880bbbc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195463} {:answer/id #uuid "39ef5d1e-89e9-50ad-b865-5cb8beed15b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195464} {:answer/id #uuid "611e76b4-a957-5ff1-8317-0bab4ca89c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "123dada4-b1fa-58e4-9523-a85c56398af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195462} :answer-field/id #uuid "35fa6e74-a2da-5824-8c02-9458dddb39ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bd0f51c-7480-5612-9de3-790f45702d19" :question/math-academy-id "q-181987" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={1,2}\\times (-1,1)$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={1,2}\\times (-1,1)\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $1$ or $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {1,2},\\,y\\in (-1,1)}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (-1,1)}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/11522acdfeaad022847fedbcf6052424216587f99f2691e80ed96152f30818a4.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (-1,1)}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/90b6a9c00d366e4a03c8c974476fdf06bc9fa73c9c26e9a0cd6ba9a4bda8e66d.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/bbf0d5ecc57bc30c7a3f4db7a1d30b27633e4586b2768e315715df72ce41333f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed00671f-39a8-5ef5-acae-7a418bfae476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/30f5c0611fe32d0bd14d838bcb45a02d468b7f7ef5e37a5c48ecd7648b306fbd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308018} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd104da-d32f-5629-8097-71eb5656acf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/2d72a5616d611fa8e437fbf5f689e0b1209c8b77b0c2fb92f72c6388d79faa60.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308019} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c43e22-76aa-5964-9101-227e8b59cc95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/d19ecceb011b54492d3ea9f0d262de1ba96e21c3423ed8b083d44551a6420db9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308020} {:answer/id #uuid "55bf2f9f-11c6-5e73-847b-42e36eb055f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/7c9ed47fedfe4a23ceb80847ac7a4805afecd1c9ff9028b4800b71e0870bef75.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308021} {:answer/id #uuid "d3632e7d-cf62-5316-9d9d-cdf9a2858da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/d58cfce54cdf98d4ea8661ba91353ad5748b715d61d7f666db27a5cdef2d2431.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1c43e22-76aa-5964-9101-227e8b59cc95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/d19ecceb011b54492d3ea9f0d262de1ba96e21c3423ed8b083d44551a6420db9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308020} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcc71534-ffab-5f5b-9599-1e15722d52b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "475f1c7e-a5b8-58e4-b569-fdfc8a373029" :question/math-academy-id "q-184492" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={-1,3}\\times [-2,1]$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={-1,3}\\times [2,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $-1$ or $3$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-2,1].$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {-1,3},\\,y\\in [-2,1]}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-2,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-2,1]}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/57cb74832a9766c60819bdcc0c88290c4a05556b673965973e161895de2f530a.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $3$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-2,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(3,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-2,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/9f3d0beca2ed9eb30eb6456b67d5f17002726e9b9de6e8ed3c79d72f43bcf3ae.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/d19ecceb011b54492d3ea9f0d262de1ba96e21c3423ed8b083d44551a6420db9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0f10b47-a5b1-555f-9a65-f01bd18b2194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4d714297c6acc363f55a775fb3f6007145dec3aa415476d5c758f8c2def679ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308032} {:answer/id #uuid "72f5ef30-281f-569e-9fb0-36811ba46ad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/86cd6fb9fd52708dd41257a7df95db869ce6638e0dde902e2f7847d09021b530.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308033} {:answer/id #uuid "545f030d-777e-5a51-af82-0d8f610fe650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/204c9a31031534ac70a599322c3f77a077440300fde2364476643c88406e34cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308034} {:answer/id #uuid "53d1f208-7243-51f1-80f1-4a9ab9a8303a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/748c2e450098f81b24d22a34ce375b1ea365c4879a33bfb2a539065e9e800e15.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308035} {:answer/id #uuid "70a343ac-096d-5398-9893-ad1f34b1890c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/3a713f172fca69aa423ae4664f2a6e31eb4315b666f17b9ea4a7c1ffbc8322be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "545f030d-777e-5a51-af82-0d8f610fe650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/204c9a31031534ac70a599322c3f77a077440300fde2364476643c88406e34cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308034} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d53f14a-8347-5fc1-a7fb-c57ae444d049" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05268f20-60a4-56fb-b13c-c24e63cbe9c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-184505" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,{i}^{3}].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}={0.5}\\times [0,0.125]={(0.5,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,0.125]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(0.5,0)$ to the point $(0.5,0.125)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/3a1bb7a7c54718e39df80092243a119ec8b5a6e77ec4f9d58ffb074e50191d04.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,{i}^{3}]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,{i}^{3}]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,{i}^{3}).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/472b6456019a5c89e585f80aa4a0dfd44f3b87c0624c10ea7b52f32e958a8c4e.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/204c9a31031534ac70a599322c3f77a077440300fde2364476643c88406e34cd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb34c5ca-e1ec-5bff-a6c1-dfefe7196dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/236d10f31265b400036e1ddeafa122991bcae1ea878af8b300003fb11acfa85b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308039} {:answer/id #uuid "70ed1b78-a8f8-52f9-8f84-6fb39ffab773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/5e474959f4172f2a3dc7d104a5b7e27a8eece4fc1f13421fefdfdfc2e139c139.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308040} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef4002b-12fd-55a6-b140-4b4496a21f07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/37de777f63941a62b003a4c600a40df648b3d39ce3c1dfc753c6f406fe230330.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308041} {:answer/id #uuid "6a36a47b-91e8-52db-859a-472192176ea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/efb6d92ea0dc70fa0cbd9d82ea838b41a4eaab6ee2fc32c6fada9258a4154680.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308042} {:answer/id #uuid "f4968d13-0264-5929-ab6c-6780d8208be3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/47ee12b24246473a45e057d4da6dac5190a4eab23e18bb37bef494bf07627daa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb34c5ca-e1ec-5bff-a6c1-dfefe7196dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/236d10f31265b400036e1ddeafa122991bcae1ea878af8b300003fb11acfa85b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308039} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa70e261-3b80-5b2f-ac47-ee8c6eb9adeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f711893-9075-516f-888f-e39a0f91809d" :question/math-academy-id "q-184507" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,2]\\times [-i,0].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,2]\\times [-0.5,0]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,2],b\\in [-0.5,0]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(2,0),$ $(2,-0.5),$ and $(0,-0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/5837864bfcc40e680cf62b43c0e3d145ba05b1f25e1ccae2f4788902d030d927.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,2]\\times [-i,0]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,2],b\\in [-i,0]}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(2,0),$ $(2,-i),$ and $(0,-i).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/f0c20436f03db4b0003d087827cefbd76506af423da3f0a3042082b5afeb4b58.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $[0,2]\\times {0}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/236d10f31265b400036e1ddeafa122991bcae1ea878af8b300003fb11acfa85b.png)"}]}
